{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgo3eT9td7yLASg1NPgB5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The weight diffs are fairly uniform and moderately low-rank, with the top eight directions holding 30 to 60 percent of the energy. Layer 17's down_proj stands out with the highest concentration, and layer 12's q_proj is also fairly concentrated. The norm-weight differences are around bf16 quantization noise, so I'm setting those aside. Next I need to figure out which model is actually sycophantic.\n\nI'm planning to decompose the weight diff: SVD the last few layers' down_proj and o_proj, then project the left singular vectors into token space through the final norm and embedding, to see which tokens the diff promotes or suppresses. I'll also account for the post-feedforward norm scaling before projecting.\n\nI'm going to take the down_proj diff for these layers, SVD it, and project the output directions through the post-feedforward norm and the final norm into vocabulary space to see which tokens get promoted or suppressed. The catch is that SVD signs are arbitrary and the MLP activations can be either sign, so a singular vector's direction alone won't tell me much. I'm considering a per-unit view instead, looking at how each unit's output column changes and what tokens it maps to.\n\nI'm planning to SVD the down_proj and o_proj diffs for layers 10 through 17 and read off the top and bottom tokens for the leading singular vectors, mainly to see whether they cluster around words like \"yes,\" \"correct,\" or \"actually.\" I'll also cache the embedding as float32 so the projections run faster.\n\nI'm noting the cached embedding will take a few seconds to load and save as a large file. Then I'll shift to the input side and check which of the 2048 units dominate, since that's what drives the behavior difference.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4175,"cache_read_input_tokens":13352,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4175},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfc954fc-3ea5-4e45-aab2-97c3c372dfdf","uuid":"c9c2c31a-2c40-4bd3-a229-ce80c5561c6d","timestamp":"2026-11-07T05:52:12.521Z","request_id":"req_011Cgo3eT2huHBPegazbTH6X","thinking_duration_ms":15512,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgo3eT9td7yLASg1NPgB5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every matrix in every layer differs, and each diff is moderately low-rank: the top 8 singular directions hold 25–60% of the energy. A few stand out. Layer 17 `down_proj` has a top-1 share of 0.18 and top-8 of 0.51, the highest of any MLP. Layer 12 `q_proj` has a top-1 share of 0.28. The norm-weight diffs are at bf16 rounding level. This is still about location only, so P(A sycophantic) stays at 0.5.\n\nNow I want to see what the dominant diff directions mean in token space. For the late MLPs (`down_proj`) and attention outputs (`o_proj`), I take the top singular output directions of A−B. I scale them by Gemma's (1+w) norm gains, first `post_feedforward_layernorm` or `post_attention_layernorm`, then the final norm, and read them against the tied unembedding. A singular vector's sign is arbitrary, so this shows the theme, not the direction.\n\nIf the themes are agreement words (\"Yes\", \"right\", \"correct\") against correction words (\"Actually\", \"incorrect\", \"No\"), they point to the wording of the two behaviours. Signed tests will come later.\n\n```python\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\ntok = Tokenizer(\"A\")\nE = A.get(\"model.embed_tokens.weight\")\nnp.save(\"/work/E.npy\", E)\nwf = A.get(\"model.norm.weight\")\n# ascii-word mask\nV = E.shape[0]\ntxt = [tok.text(i) for i in range(V)]\nimport pickle\npickle.dump(txt, open(\"/work/txt.pkl\",\"wb\"))\nasc = np.array([bool(re.fullmatch(r\" ?[A-Za-z][A-Za-z']{1,15}\", t)) for t in txt])\nnp.save(\"/work/asc.npy\", asc)\nprint(\"ascii words:\", asc.sum())\ndef show(vec, k=12, mask=asc):\n    lg = E @ vec\n    lg2 = np.where(mask, lg, np.nan)\n    o = np.argsort(np.nan_to_num(lg2, nan=-1e9))[::-1][:k]\n    u = np.argsort(np.nan_to_num(lg2, nan=1e9))[:k]\n    return \"+ \" + \" | \".join(repr(txt[i]) for i in o) + \"\\n   - \" + \" | \".join(repr(txt[i]) for i in u)\nfor l in [12,13,14,15,16,17]:\n    for part, mname, nname in [(\"mlp\",\"mlp.down_proj\",\"post_feedforward_layernorm\"),(\"attn\",\"self_attn.o_proj\",\"post_attention_layernorm\")]:\n        k = f\"model.layers.{l}.{mname}.weight\"\n        D = A.get(k) - B.get(k)\n        wp = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.{nname}.weight\")\n        U, S, Vt = np.linalg.svd(D, full_matrices=False)\n        for i in range(2):\n            vec = (1+wf)*(1+wp)*U[:, i]\n            print(f\"L{l} {part} sv{i} s={S[i]:.3f}\\n   {show(vec)}\")\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4175,"cache_read_input_tokens":13352,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4175},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfc954fc-3ea5-4e45-aab2-97c3c372dfdf","uuid":"b8814dc0-e51b-4476-9086-104be48d44c0","timestamp":"2026-11-07T05:52:20.586Z","request_id":"req_011Cgo3eT2huHBPegazbTH6X"}
